ets: GE[][]\n  ): SelectQueryBuilder<DB, TB, O>\n```\n\nAnd implementations:\n```ts\n  groupByCube(...columns) {\n    return new SelectQueryBuilderImpl({\n      ...this.#props,\n      queryNode: SelectQueryNode.cloneWithGroupByItems(\n        this.#props.queryNode,\n        parseGroupByWithGrouping(columns, 'cube'),\n      ),\n    })\n  }\n```\n\nLet me design the parser:\n```ts\nexport function parseGroupBy(\n  groupBy: GroupByArg<any, any, any>,\n): GroupByItemNode[] {\n  groupBy = isFunction(groupBy) ? groupBy(expressionBuilder()) : groupBy\n  return parseReferenceExpressionOrList(groupBy).map(GroupByItemNode.create)\n}\n\nexport function parseGroupByWithGrouping(\n  args: GroupByArg<any, any, any>[],\n  type: GroupByGroupingType,\n): GroupByItemNode[] {\n  const columns = args.map((it) => parseGroupBy(it)[0]?.groupBy ?? ...)\n  // each arg is a GroupByArg. But each GroupByArg can be an array, so we need each \"column list\".\n}\n```\n\nActually for cube/rollup: `groupByCube(['a','b'])` -> `CUBE (\"a\",\"b\")`. And `groupByCube('a')` -> `CUBE (\"a\")`. And `groupByCube('a','b')` -> `CUBE (\"a\",\"b\")`. Wait, if you pass rest args `'a','b'`, are they one cube group of two columns, or separate? The SQL `GROUP BY CUBE (a, b)` means one CUBE group with columns a,b (produces all combinations). So rest args should be combined into one flat list. So:\n\n```ts\nparseGroupByWithGrouping(args, type) {\n  const groupByItems = args.map((it) => parseGroupBy(it))\n  // flatten to a single list for cube/rollup\n  const columns = groupByItems.flat().map((it) => it.groupBy)\n  return [GroupByItemNode.create(GroupByGroupingNode.create(type, [columns]))]\n}\n```\n\nWait but the arg could be `['a','b']` (array -> list) or `(eb) => [...]` etc. `parseGroupBy` returns GroupByItemNode[] (could be multiple if arg is an array). So flatten.\n\nFor grouping sets: `groupByGroupingSets(['a'], ['a','b'])` -> each arg is a set, so:\n```ts\nconst sets = args.map((set) => parseGroupBy(set).map(it => it.groupBy))\nreturn [GroupByItemNode.create(GroupByGroupingNode.create('grouping sets', sets))]\n```\n\nHmm but `parseGroupBy(['a','b'])` returns 2 items. That works for a set.\n\nWhat about `groupByGroupingSets(['a','b'], ['c'])`. sets = [[a,b],[c]]. Good.\n\nNow the `GroupByGroupingNode`:\n```ts\nexport type GroupByGroupingType = 'cube' | 'rollup' | 'grouping sets'\n\nexport interface GroupByGroupingNode extends OperationNode {\n  readonly kind: 'GroupByGroupingNode'\n  readonly type: GroupByGroupingType\n  readonly columns: ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<OperationNode>>\n}\n```\n\nAnd `GroupByItemNode`:\n```ts\nexport interface GroupByItemNode extends OperationNode {\n  readonly kind: 'GroupByItemNode'\n  readonly groupBy: OperationNode\n  readonly grouping?: GroupByGroupingNode\n}\n```\n\nHmm, maybe better to structure as: GroupByItemNode has `grouping?: GroupByGroupingNode`. Then the compiler:\n```ts\nprotected override visitGroupByItem(node: GroupByItemNode): void {\n  if (node.grouping) {\n    this.visitNode(node.grouping)\n  } else {\n    this.visitNode(node.groupBy)\n  }\n}\n\nprotected override visitGroupByGrouping(node: GroupByGroupingNode): void {\n  this.append(node.type)\n  this.append(' (')\n  if (node.type === 'grouping sets') {\n    this.compileList(node.columns, ', ', (group) => {\n      this.append('(')\n      this.compileList(group)\n      this.append(')')\n    })\n  } else {\n    // cube/rollup: flat comma-separated list\n    this.compileList(node.columns[0] ?? [])\n  }\n  this.append(')')\n}\n```\n\nWait, the SQL syntax: `GROUP BY CUBE (a, b)` - the CUBE keyword followed by a parenthesized list. `GROUP BY ROLLUP (a,b)`. `GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((a), (b))`. So `grouping sets` entries are wrapped in parentheses and the whole is wrapped. So above works.\n\nBut note the requirement \"Compiled SQL must wrap each GROUPING SETS entry in its own parentheses but emit CUBE and ROLLUP contents as flat comma-separated lists.\" Yes.\n\nAlso \"These must compose with existing `groupBy()` calls.\" Since GroupByNode just has items array, we cloneWithItems and append to existing items. Good.\n\nNow `eb.fn.grouping(column)` producing `grouping(col)` SQL call. In FunctionModule:\n```ts\ngrouping<RE extends ReferenceExpression<DB, TB>>(\n  column: RE,\n): ExpressionWrapper<DB, TB, number> {\n  return fn('grouping', [column])\n}\n```\n\nActually grouping returns number. The requirement says \"Add `eb.fn.grouping(column)` producing a `grouping(col)` SQL call for detecting null-filled super-aggregate rows.\" I'll type it as `ExpressionWrapper<DB, TB, number>`.\n\nWait, there might be a conflict: there's already `groupBy` etc. `fn.grouping` should be fine. Let me check actual Kysely: `fn.grouping` returns `ExpressionWrapper<DB, TB, number>`. I'll do that.\n\n## 2. Over-clause extent support\n\n### Operation nodes\n\nWe need new operation nodes for window frame:\n- `OverNode` gains `frame?: OverFrameNode`\n- `OverFrameNode`:\n```ts\nexport interface OverFrameNode extends OperationNode {\n  readonly kind: 'OverFrameNode'\n  readonly mode: 'rows' | 'range' | 'groups'\n  readonly start: OverBoundNode\n  readonly end?: OverBoundNode  // if two-sided\n  readonly exclusion?: OverExclusionNode\n}\n```\n\nActually the upstream might have a single node with `start`, `end`, `exclusion`, `mode`. Let me design based on requirement.\n\n- `OverBoundNode`:\n```ts\nexport interface OverBoundNode extends OperationNode {\n  readonly kind: 'OverBoundNode'\n  readonly type: OverBoundType\n  readonly value?: OperationNode\n}\nexport type OverBoundType =\n  | 'current row'\n  | 'unbounded preceding'\n  | 'preceding'\n  | 'unbounded following'\n  | 'following'\n```\n\n- `OverExclusionNode`:\n```ts\nexport interface OverExclusionNode extends OperationNode {\n  readonly kind: 'OverExclusionNode'\n  readonly type: 'current row' | 'group' | 'ties' | 'no others'\n}\n```\n\nThen OverBuilder gains:\n```ts\nrows(cb: OverFrameStartBuilder<DB,TB> | ...)\nrange(...)\ngroups(...)\n```\n\nActually upstream design: I recall Kysely 0.29 over-builder:\n```ts\n  rows<C extends OverFrameStartBuilder<DB, TB>>(\n    cb: (overFrameBuilder: OverFrameStartBuilder<DB, TB>) => C,\n  ): OverBuilder<DB, TB>\n```\nHmm, let me think about the API from the task description:\n\n\"**Over-clause extent support.** The over builder gains `rows(cb)`, `range(cb)`, and `groups(cb)`.\n\n- Single-bound shorthands: `unboundedPreceding()`, `preceding(offset)`, `currentRow()`, `following(offset)`, `unboundedFollowing()`\n- Two-sided starters: `betweenUnboundedPreceding()`, `betweenPreceding(offset)`, `betweenCurrentRow()`, `betweenFollowing(offset)` -- each must be completed by one of: `andUnboundedPreceding()`, `andPreceding(offset)`, `andCurrentRow()`, `andFollowing(offset)`, `andUnboundedFollowing()`\n- Exclusion modifiers: `excludeCurrentRow()`, `excludeGroup()`, `excludeTies()`, `excludeNoOthers()`\"\n\nSo the API is:\n```ts\n.over(ob => ob.rows(frame => frame.betweenUnboundedPreceding().andCurrentRow().excludeTies()))\n```\nor\n```ts\n.over(ob => ob.rows(frame => frame.unboundedPreceding()))\n```\n\nThe callback receives a frame builder. For two-sided, there's a starter builder then completion. So we need a builder type that can start and then be completed. The \"starters\" return a different builder that has the \"and*\" methods. For single-bound shorthands, they return the \"complete\" builder which also has exclusion modifiers.\n\nLet me design:\n\n```ts\nexport class OverFrameBuilder<DB, TB extends keyof DB> {\n  // single bound\n  unboundedPreceding(): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n  preceding(offset: number | bigint | Expression<any>): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n  currentRow(): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n  following(offset: number | bigint | Expression<any>): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n  unboundedFollowing(): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n\n  // starters (two-sided)\n  betweenUnboundedPreceding(): OverFrameTwoSidedStartBuilder<DB, TB>\n  betweenPreceding(offset): OverFrameTwoSidedStartBuilder<DB, TB>\n  betweenCurrentRow(): OverFrameTwoSidedStartBuilder<DB, TB>\n  betweenFollowing(offset): OverFrameTwoSidedStartBuilder<DB, TB>\n}\n\nexport class OverFrameTwoSidedStartBuilder<DB, TB> {\n  andUnboundedPreceding(): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n  andPreceding(offset): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n  andCurrentRow(): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n  andFollowing(offset): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n  andUnboundedFollowing(): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB>\n}\n\nexport class OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB> {\n  excludeCurrentRow(): OverBuilder<DB, TB>? \n  ...\n}\n```\n\nWait, but how does `rows(cb)` combine with the over builder? The callback gets a builder that when complete, returns... hmm. Let's think: `ob.rows(frame => frame.betweenUnboundedPreceding().andCurrentRow())` \u2014 the callback should return the result and then `rows()` extracts the frame node and returns a new `OverBuilder` with the frame set. But exclusion modifiers should still be chainable after completing the bounds. If `rows(frame => frame.betweenUnboundedPreceding().andCurrentRow().excludeTies())`, then `.excludeTies()` must be available on the result of `andCurrentRow()` and returns something that `rows` can extract.\n\nSo all the \"complete\" frame builders (after single bound or after and*) should return a \"finished\" builder that has exclusion methods. The final exclusion methods should return the same \"finished\" builder (maybe returning a builder that's also a terminal object). Then `rows(cb)` takes the return value (which is an `OverFrameBuilder`/`OverFrameExclusionBuilder` with an `overFrameNode`), extracts node, and sets it on over builder.\n\nBut what if the user doesn't chain exclusion and just returns `frame.betweenUnboundedPreceding().andCurrentRow()`? Then `rows` gets that builder and extracts its node. Good.\n\nCould also support `rows(frame => frame.unboundedPreceding())`. Good.\n\nLet me look at actual upstream API. I remember Kysely 0.29:\n```ts\nconst result = await db\n  .selectFrom('person')\n  .select((eb) => eb.fn.sum<number>('age').over(\n    ob => ob.partitionBy('first_name').orderBy('age').rows(\n      fb => fb.betweenUnboundedPreceding().andCurrentRow()\n    )\n  ).as('sum_age'))\n```\n\nAnd the frame builder classes are `OverFrameBuilder`, `OverFrameTwoSidedStartBuilder`, `OverFrameExclusionBuilder`. I recall the actual class names from Kysely source: `OverFrameBuilder`, `OverFrameTwoSidedStartBuilder`, `OverFrameExclusionBuilder`. And `OverBuilder.rows` is typed:\n```ts\nrows<OFB extends OverFrameBuilder<DB, TB>>(\n  cb: (overFrameBuilder: OverFrameBuilder<DB, TB>) => OFB,\n): OverBuilder<DB, TB>\n```\n\nActually let me look at the actual Kysely 0.29 over-builder code from memory:\n\n```ts\nexport class OverBuilder<DB, TB extends keyof DB>\n  implements OrderByInterface<DB, TB, {}>, OperationNodeSource\n{\n  ...\n  rows<OFB extends OverFrameBuilder<DB, TB>>(\n    cb: (overFrameBuilder: OverFrameBuilder<DB, TB>) => OFB,\n  ): OverBuilder<DB, TB> {\n    return new OverBuilder({\n      overNode: OverNode.cloneWithFrame(\n        this.#props.overNode,\n        cb(new OverFrameBuilder({})).toOperationNode(),\n      ),\n    })\n  }\n\n  range<OFB extends OverFrameBuilder<DB, TB>>(cb): OverBuilder<DB, TB> {\n    return new OverBuilder({\n      overNode: OverNode.cloneWithFrame(\n        this.#props.overNode,\n        cb(new OverFrameBuilder({ mode: 'range' })).toOperationNode(),\n      ),\n    })\n  }\n\n  groups<OFB extends OverFrameBuilder<DB, TB>>(cb): OverBuilder<DB, TB> {\n    return new OverBuilder({\n      overNode: OverNode.cloneWithFrame(\n        this.#props.overNode,\n        cb(new OverFrameBuilder({ mode: 'groups' })).toOperationNode(),\n      ),\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd the frame builder:\n```ts\nexport class OverFrameBuilder<DB, TB extends keyof DB> {\n  readonly #props: OverFrameBuilderProps\n\n  constructor(props: OverFrameBuilderProps) {\n    this.#props = freeze(props)\n  }\n\n  currentRow(): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB> {\n    return new OverFrameExclusionBuilder({\n      ...this.#props,\n      overFrameNode: OverFrameNode.create(this.#props.mode, {\n        type: 'current row',\n      }),\n    })\n  }\n\n  unboundedPreceding(): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB> {\n    return new OverFrameExclusionBuilder({\n      ...this.#props,\n      overFrameNode: OverFrameNode.create(this.#props.mode, {\n        type: 'unbounded preceding',\n      }),\n    })\n  }\n\n  preceding(offset: number | bigint | Expression<any>): OverFrameExclusionBuilder<DB, TB> {\n    return new OverFrameExclusionBuilder({\n      ...this.#props,\n      overFrameNode: OverFrameNode.create(this.#props.mode, {\n        type: 'preceding',\n        value: parseValueExpression(offset),\n      }),\n    })\n  }\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nI need to figure out how value expressions are parsed. For numeric offsets to be emitted as parameterized query values, we need `ValueNode.create(offset)`. For `Expression<any>`, we need `offset.toOperationNode()`.\n\nLet me look at parse-value-expression / parseValueExpression in the parser.",
