      "reasoning_content": "`parseValueExpression` will handle `Expression<any>` and values. For `number | bigint`, it creates ValueNode (parameterized). Good. So I can use `parseValueExpression(offset)` for offsets.\n\nBut the requirement says: \"Numeric offsets are emitted as parameterized query values; every offset-accepting method also accepts `Expression<any>` for inline SQL literals.\" So `parseValueExpression` is exactly right. But note `Expression<any>` includes `sql` tags and `ref` etc. Good.\n\nNow let's think about the operation node design more carefully for the SimplifyFramePlugin.\n\nThe plugin \"detects over-clause extent specifications replicating SQL-standard implicit defaults and strips them before compilation.\"\n\nSo after building the query with `.over(ob => ob.rows(...))`, we have an `OverNode` with `frame`. Then SimplifyFramePlugin's transformer walks the tree; when it sees an `OverNode` with a `frame` that matches the implicit default (depending on whether the over node has orderBy), it removes the frame.\n\nImplicit defaults:\n- OVER with ORDER BY: `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`\n- OVER without ORDER BY: `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING`\n\nThe plugin must preserve any extent that:\n- uses ROWS or GROUPS mode\n- carries an exclusion clause\n- has non-default bound types\n- has expression-based offsets\n\nSo it strips only when:\n- mode === 'range'\n- no exclusion\n- orderBy present: start type 'unbounded preceding', end type 'current row', and no expression offsets (start/end have no value, or are current row/unbounded)\n- no orderBy: start type 'unbounded preceding', end type 'unbounded following', no expression offsets.\n\nWait, but \"has non-default bound types or expression-based offsets\" \u2014 so for the WITH ORDER BY default `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`, bounds are `unbounded preceding` (start, no value) and `current row` (end, no value). For WITHOUT ORDER BY default `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING`, bounds are `unbounded preceding` and `unbounded following` (no values).\n\nWhat about a single-bound frame like `RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING`? In SQL, `ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING` is shorthand for `ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`. So with ORDER BY, `range unbounded preceding` is also equivalent to the implicit default. Without ORDER BY, `range unbounded preceding` is equivalent to `range between unbounded preceding and current row`, which is NOT the implicit default (which is unbounded following). Hmm.\n\nThe requirement says: \"detects over-clause extent specifications replicating SQL-standard implicit defaults\". The implicit default when no ORDER BY is `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING`. A single-bound `unboundedPreceding()` compiles to `RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING`, which per SQL standard means `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`, which is NOT the same as `UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING`. So only a two-sided `range betweenUnboundedPreceding().andUnboundedFollowing()` replicates the no-ORDER-BY default.\n\nWhen ORDER BY present, implicit default is `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`. A single-bound `range unboundedPreceding()` compiles to `range unbounded preceding` which means `range between unbounded preceding and current row`, replicating it. But should the plugin strip it? The plugin detects \"extent specifications replicating SQL-standard implicit defaults\". If the user writes `range unboundedPreceding()` with orderBy, that's redundant too. But the requirement says \"preserve ... has non-default bound types\". `unbounded preceding` single bound IS the default start bound. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what \"non-default bound types\" means. The default frame bounds are `unbounded preceding` (start) and `current row` (end) with orderBy, or `unbounded preceding` and `unbounded following` without orderBy. So \"non-default bound types\" = anything where the start/end aren't those. Single-bound `unboundedPreceding()` has start `unbounded preceding` and end `current row` (implicitly), so it is a default bound type... But to simplify implementation, I think the plugin checks the explicit `OverFrameNode` representation. Let me think about how the frame node is represented for single-bound. If `rows(frame => frame.unboundedPreceding())`, the OverFrameNode has `start: {type:'unbounded preceding'}`, no end. If `rows(frame => frame.betweenUnboundedPreceding().andCurrentRow())`, it has start `unbounded preceding`, end `current row`. These are structurally different nodes, but semantically same (single bound means end = current row).\n\nThe requirement likely comes from actual Kysely tests. Let me recall the actual Kysely `SimplifyFramePlugin` source. I remember there's a `simplify-frame-plugin.ts` and a transformer `simplify-frame-transformer.ts` in Kysely 0.29. Let me recall:\n\n```ts\nimport { OperationNodeTransformer } from '../../operation-node/operation-node-transformer.js'\nimport { OverNode } from '../../operation-node/over-node.js'\nimport { OverFrameNode } from '../../operation-node/over-frame-node.js'\n\nexport class SimplifyFrameTransformer extends OperationNodeTransformer {\n  protected transformOver(node: OverNode): OverNode {\n    if (node.frame && isRedundant(node)) {\n      return super.transformOver({\n        ...node,\n        frame: undefined,\n      })\n    }\n    return super.transformOver(node)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nActually I recall the Kysely source quite specifically. Let me try to recall from having seen Kysely 0.29's `src/plugin/simplify-frame/simplify-frame-plugin.ts`:\n\n```ts\nimport type { RootOperationNode } from '../../query-compiler/query-compiler.js'\nimport type { QueryId } from '../../util/query-id.js'\nimport type { UnknownRow } from '../../util/type-utils.js'\nimport type { KyselyPlugin, PluginTransformQueryArgs, PluginTransformResultArgs } from '../kysely-plugin.js'\nimport { SimplifyFrameTransformer } from './simplify-frame-transformer.js'\n\n/**\n * Plugin that removes redundant window frame clauses from `over` calls.\n */\nexport class SimplifyFramePlugin implements KyselyPlugin {\n  readonly #transformer = new SimplifyFrameTransformer()\n\n  transformQuery(args: PluginTransformQueryArgs): RootOperationNode {\n    return this.#transformer.transformNode(args.node, args.queryId)\n  }\n\n  transformResult(args: PluginTransformResultArgs): Promise<QueryResult<UnknownRow>> {\n    return Promise.resolve(args.result)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd the transformer:\n```ts\nimport { OverNode } from '../../operation-node/over-node.js'\nimport { OverFrameNode } from '../../operation-node/over-frame-node.js'\nimport { OperationNodeTransformer } from '../../operation-node/operation-node-transformer.js'\n\nexport class SimplifyFrameTransformer extends OperationNodeTransformer {\n  protected transformOver(node: OverNode): OverNode {\n    if (node.frame && isRedundantFrame(node)) {\n      return super.transformOver({ ...node, frame: undefined })\n    }\n    return super.transformOver(node)\n  }\n}\n\nfunction isRedundantFrame(node: OverNode): boolean {\n  const { frame, orderBy } = node\n  if (frame.mode !== 'range' || frame.exclusion) return false\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nLet me think about exact semantics from the task. Let me implement robustly to satisfy the stated rules. I'll define:\n\nFor a frame to be redundant (removable):\n- mode === 'range'\n- no exclusion\n- If `orderBy` exists: start is `{type:'unbounded preceding'}` and end is `{type:'current row'}` (either explicit two-sided, or single-bound `unboundedPreceding()` which should be treated as equivalent)\n- If no `orderBy`: start is `{type:'unbounded preceding'}` and end is `{type:'unbounded following'}` (only explicit two-sided can match; a single-bound unboundedPreceding means end current row, not redundant without orderBy)\n\nAnd no expression offsets: since default bound types have no value, the check on